---
import CodeExcerpt from './CodeExcerpt.astro';
import LifeReload from './LifeReload.astro';
const migration=String.raw`spec related :: StateV1 -> StateV2 -> Prop
spec related old next =
  Eq old.value (next.base + next.delta)

migrate :: StateV1 %1 -> StateV2
migrate old =
  StateV2 { base = old.value, delta = 0 }`;
---
<section class="evolution-section language-section" id="live-development" aria-labelledby="evolution-title">
  <div class="tour-heading"><h2 id="evolution-title">Instant rebuilds.<br />Live upgrades.</h2><p>Strong type systems feel miserable if every small edit makes you wait thirty seconds for a rebuild. Cinch hashes each function’s interface separately from its body so incremental builds stay near-instant, and uses the same dependency graph for live hot reload in development and checked live upgrades in production.</p></div>
  <div class="incremental-layout"><div class="incremental-copy"><h3>Recompile only what<br />your edit changes.</h3><p>Every module saves a compact <code>.cinchif</code> summary with separate hashes for its public contract and its body. Edit how a function works without changing its signature, and nothing that imports it has to recompile.</p><p>When a signature does change, only the declarations that actually use it rebuild. Object code caches per declaration and compiles in parallel across CPU cores.</p><a class="text-link" href="/reference/18-artifacts-and-incremental/">How incremental compilation works <span>↗</span></a></div>
    <LifeReload />
  </div>
  <div class="hot-reload-layout"><div><h3>Upgrade a running service<br />without dropping state.</h3><p>Upgrading a live service usually means draining connections or risking mismatched state. Here a running counter moves from one stored field to a base plus a delta: <code>migrate</code> takes <code>StateV1 %1</code> (so the old state is consumed once and can never be reused by mistake), while <code>related</code> checks that the total count stays the same.</p><p>New requests route immediately to version 2 while in-flight calls and callbacks finish safely on version 1 before the old code unloads.</p><a class="text-link" href="/learn/21-upgrades-and-contracts/">Upgrades and temporal contracts tutorial <span>↗</span></a></div><CodeExcerpt code={migration} label="COUNTER / STATE MIGRATION" caption="The relation preserves old.value = next.base + next.delta."/></div>
  <div class="activation-controls"><button type="button" class="gadget-key" data-upgrade-run>Run an upgrade <span aria-hidden="true">▸</span></button><div class="activation-rail" aria-hidden="true"><i data-upgrade-rail></i></div><p class="mono" data-upgrade-status aria-live="polite">Version 1 is serving every call.</p></div>
  <ol class="activation-sequence" data-upgrade-steps aria-label="Checked module activation sequence"><li><span class="mono">01 / PREPARE</span><h4>Verify the update set.</h4><p>Check the new module, its state migration, and every specialized dependent that must move with it.</p></li><li><span class="mono">02 / COORDINATE</span><h4>Reach a safe boundary.</h4><p>Wait for a declared transition point or run adjacent versions side by side while active calls drain.</p></li><li><span class="mono">03 / ACTIVATE</span><h4>Migrate and switch.</h4><p>Run the linear state migration, transfer resource ownership, and route new calls to version 2.</p></li><li><span class="mono">04 / RETIRE</span><h4>Unload old code.</h4><p>Reclaim version 1 automatically once the last in-flight frame returns.</p></li></ol>
  <a class="text-link" href="/reference/17-upgrades-and-temporal-contracts/">Upgrades and temporal contracts reference <span>↗</span></a>
</section>
<script>
  const evolution=document.querySelector('#live-development');
  // The activation protocol, one stage at a time.
  const upgradeButton=evolution?.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-upgrade-run]');
  const steps=evolution?.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-upgrade-steps] > li');
  const rail=evolution?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-upgrade-rail]');
  const status=evolution?.querySelector('[data-upgrade-status]');
  const messages=['Checking the update set: artifacts, compatibility evidence, and dependents.','Waiting for a valid boundary. Calls in progress keep running version 1.','State migrated and ownership moved. New calls go to version 2.','The last version 1 call has returned. Version 1 is unloaded.'];
  const calm=()=>matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
  upgradeButton?.addEventListener('click',async()=>{
    if(!steps||!rail||!status)return;
    upgradeButton.disabled=true;
    steps.forEach(step=>step.classList.remove('is-active','is-done'));
    for(let i=0;i<steps.length;i++){
      steps[i].classList.add('is-active');
      rail.style.width=`${((i+1)/steps.length)*100}%`;
      status.textContent=messages[i];
      await new Promise(resolve=>setTimeout(resolve,calm()?0:1100));
      steps[i].classList.replace('is-active','is-done');
    }
    upgradeButton.disabled=false;
    upgradeButton.firstChild!.textContent='Run it again ';
  });
</script>
